@startuml
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam ArrowColor black
skinparam nodesep 40
skinparam ranksep 50
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam rectangle {
  BackgroundColor white
  BorderColor #90A4AE
  FontColor #455A64
}
skinparam card {
  BackgroundColor #E8F4FD
  BorderColor #2196F3
  FontColor #1565C0
}

title ttt — one board, four ways of looking at it\nX has played cell 1, O has played cell 3

card memory [
**1. In the server's memory** — the ""Game"" that ""SessionController"" owns
====
|= index |<#ECEFF1> 0 |<#ECEFF1> 1 |<#ECEFF1> 2 |<#ECEFF1> 3 |<#ECEFF1> 4 |<#ECEFF1> 5 |<#ECEFF1> 6 |<#ECEFF1> 7 |<#ECEFF1> 8 |
|= cell |= 1 |= 2 |= 3 |= 4 |= 5 |= 6 |= 7 |= 8 |= 9 |
|= ""board"" |<#BBDEFB> ""Some(X)"" |<#FFFFFF> ""None"" |<#C8E6C9> ""Some(O)"" |<#FFFFFF> ""None"" |<#FFFFFF> ""None"" |<#FFFFFF> ""None"" |<#FFFFFF> ""None"" |<#FFFFFF> ""None"" |<#FFFFFF> ""None"" |
----
""board: [Option<Player>; 9]"" — nine slots, each one taken or free.
The array starts at 0, the cells are numbered from 1: ""cell(n)"" is ""board[n - 1]"".
""turn"" and ""state"" sit beside it in the same struct.
]

card text [
**2. As nine characters** — what ""wire_state()"" produces
====
|= cell |= 1 |= 2 |= 3 |= 4 |= 5 |= 6 |= 7 |= 8 |= 9 |
|= char |<#BBDEFB> ""X"" |<#FFFFFF> ""2"" |<#C8E6C9> ""O"" |<#FFFFFF> ""4"" |<#FFFFFF> ""5"" |<#FFFFFF> ""6"" |<#FFFFFF> ""7"" |<#FFFFFF> ""8"" |<#FFFFFF> ""9"" |
----
= ""X2O456789""
----
A taken cell shows **X** or **O**. A free cell shows **its own number**,
so the board is its own legend: what you read is what you type.
A fresh board is therefore ""123456789"".
]

card frame [
**3. On the wire** — the board is the argument of a ""STATE"" frame
====
""STATE X2O456789<U+005C>n""
----
16 bytes. Notice what is **not** there: no seat, no whose-turn,
no score, no length field. The newline ends it.
]

card screen [
**4. On the player's screen** — after ""from_wire_state"" builds a throwaway ""Game""
====
"" X | 2 | O ""
""<U+002D><U+002D><U+002D>+<U+002D><U+002D><U+002D>+<U+002D><U+002D><U+002D>""
"" 4 | 5 | 6 ""
""<U+002D><U+002D><U+002D>+<U+002D><U+002D><U+002D>+<U+002D><U+002D><U+002D>""
"" 7 | 8 | 9 ""
----
""ConsoleDisplay"" draws it and forgets it. Whose turn it is was
never sent: it is **counted** — one X and one O, equal, and X starts,
so it is X's turn. One source of truth, on the server.
]

memory -down-> text : ""game.wire_state()""
text -down-> frame : ""encode_update(&Update::Board(cells))""
frame -down-> screen : ""decode_update"" then ""Game::from_wire_state""

note right of frame
  **Why send the whole board every time,**
  **instead of just the move?**
  Because then a client can never drift out of
  step. It holds no game state of its own — it
  draws what it is given. A confused client just
  asks ""STATE"" again and is correct once more.
end note

@enduml
